    <section id="correlation" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05.2 · independent emitters</span>
        <h2>The W3C trace id is the only correlation identifier.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="telemetry-emitters">
        <article><small>emitter 01</small><strong><code>dekopond</code></strong><span>receipt · session · model · shell</span></article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">traceparent →</i>
        <article><small>emitter 02</small><strong><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></strong><span>decision · provider · HTTP</span></article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">OTLP ↘</i>
        <article><small>backend</small><strong>OpenObserve</strong><span>joins by trace and parent IDs</span></article>
      </div>

      <p>Every proposal carries a mandatory W3C <code>traceParent</code>; the broker parents its spans on it and stamps its audit records with the trace id. A gateway that exports nothing still mints a sampled context from OS entropy, so an exporting broker behind it records every decision. Invocations are numbered <code>&lt;trace&gt;-&lt;counter&gt;</code>.</p>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>strict</span><h3>Malformed context</h3><p>Refused at decode.</p></article>
        <article><span>never</span><h3>Third parties</h3><p>No <code>traceparent</code> header reaches a provider’s endpoint.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="signals" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05.3 · one job per signal</span>
        <h2>Audit, traces, and logs answer different questions.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Broker audit</span></div><h3>What was authorized?</h3><p>One log record per decision, <code>broker.decision</code> or <code>broker.execution</code>, inside the span that made it. Stdout JSON always; OTLP when <code>telemetry</code> names a receiver.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>OTel traces</span></div><h3>What ran, and where did time go?</h3><p>Duration, parentage, and the path from chat to HTTP. No span is dropped; an oversized attribute is truncated with a marker.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>OTel logs</span></div><h3>What was said and run?</h3><p>Prompts, answers, scripts, output, accounting, refusals.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Lose the exporter, lose the audit.</strong>
        <p>There is no on-disk audit sink: audit past a pod restart means configuring <code>telemetry</code>, and a collector reachable only through a proxy is unreachable. Nothing here detects tampering: your telemetry store is inside your trust boundary.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="logs" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05.4 · logs that earn their storage</span>
        <h2>A span measures time; a log preserves a fact.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Record</th><th scope="col">Fields</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>broker.decision</code> · <code>broker.execution</code></th><td>identity, capability, decision, policy ids and digest, sanitized HTTP calls</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.model.prompt</code> · <code>agent.model.answer</code></th><td>the messages appended since the last turn, in full</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>agent.tool.script</code> · <code>agent.tool.output</code></th><td>the script text and its bounded output</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>accounting.model.turn</code> · <code>accounting.http.request</code></th><td>duration, token usage, method, authority, status, bytes</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">refusal and error events</th><td>a stable category and bounded ids</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>No start/completed log pairs: a span already has those.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05.5 · two exclusions</span>
        <h2>Secret bytes are the only exclusion.</h2>
        <p>Dekopon is the operator’s agent system, not the end user’s private one: talk to a Dekopon agent and you’re talking to its operator.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Always exported</th><th scope="col">Never exported</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>chat messages, prompts, and model answers</td><td>provider credentials and DRN-resolved secrets</td></tr>
            <tr><td>model-authored scripts and tool output</td><td>the gateway’s own chat tokens, model keys, and OTLP headers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>every command word, with its exit code</td><td>HTTP request and response headers and bodies</td></tr>
            <tr><td>proposal input, provider output digest, <code>url.full</code></td><td>attachment bytes, which bypass the model entirely</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p><code>shell.command</code> counts argument values rather than recording them: a <code>curl -d</code> body is secret bytes wearing argv’s clothes. Recording them is the committed direction; storage-backed spans carry the namespace token, never scope or content.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="openobserve" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>05.6 · open standards on small hardware</span>
        <h2>Send OTLP to the backend you choose.</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:5080/api/default
<span class="code-purple">export</span> OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS='Authorization=Basic%20[REDACTED],organization=default,stream-name=dekopon'

<span class="code-comment"># Each process exports its own leg.</span>
dekopond --config /etc/dekopon/dekopond.yaml
dekopon-brokerd --config /etc/dekopon/broker.yaml</code></pre>

      <p>OTLP over HTTP or gRPC, logs and spans from one <code>telemetry</code> block. The OpenObserve smoke test starts a real receiver, runs a provider, and finds the decision record and its spans under one trace id.</p>

